                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "78496872-fc58-388f-a521-081873df2f94",
                        "entry_id": "1f4735ba-0a71-36cf-b988-6cbc15860024",
                        "glosses": [
                            "cecha tego, co zagadkowe"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f7d5ad69-38fb-4fbf-a543-64ae4153de4a",
                                "sense_id": "78496872-fc58-388f-a521-081873df2f94",
                                "text": "Zagadkowość postaci polskiego arystokraty wzmacnia jego niejasna przeszłość i zbrodnie w otoczeniu hrabiego."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1.1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f9c6bdd0-efbf-46b1-977a-2f13f356673d",
                        "source_entry_id": "1f4735ba-0a71-36cf-b988-6cbc15860024",
                        "source_sense_id": "78496872-fc58-388f-a521-081873df2f94",
                        "target_lang_word": "ambiguity",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "ambiguity",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "aefef315-5542-39eb-887c-cd977f8811a3",
                                        "entry_id": "fe856398-c0d8-3ac5-a71a-62063c91bdf1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The state of being ambiguous; the state of leaving room for more than one interpretation."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9dba432b-8b82-4b83-bf66-4f9bf56cfaa0",
                                                "sense_id": "aefef315-5542-39eb-887c-cd977f8811a3",
                                                "text": "His speech was made with such great ambiguity that neither supporter nor opponent could be certain of his true position."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d5592aea-f392-41da-80f7-68466a37aed2",
                                                "sense_id": "aefef315-5542-39eb-887c-cd977f8811a3",
                                                "text": "Whan this ſayd monument diſcouered was / Suche a ſuauite and fragrant odoure / Aſcended from the corps by ſingular grace / Paſſyng all worldly ſwetnes and ſauour / That all there present that day and hour / Suppoſed they had ben / in the felicite / Of erthely paradiſe / without ambiguite."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "307b5f88-5d72-3050-b802-d44c87e5efdd",
                                        "entry_id": "fe856398-c0d8-3ac5-a71a-62063c91bdf1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An instance of this state: words or statements that are open to more than one interpretation, explanation or meaning, especially if that meaning cannot be determined from the context."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "44f83797-11ca-4ac8-a0a0-d4b938a66e69",
                                                "sense_id": "307b5f88-5d72-3050-b802-d44c87e5efdd",
                                                "text": "If two persons bore the same name, and confusion was likely to be caused, ambiguity was avoided by adding the name of the father[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c305ed61-1f5b-4e4c-9544-528f661375b9",
                                                "sense_id": "307b5f88-5d72-3050-b802-d44c87e5efdd",
                                                "text": "For people who would rather face a plague of locusts than permit an avoidable ambiguity, this is like having their underwear twisted. You may think we should say to such people, “Get a life.” By all means tell them that. I’m neither making these rules up nor trying to enforce them; my job in this book is to point out to you what seems to be the current state of the language and its speakers.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6ebbfdb9-b2e7-4b73-a0c1-684a430dedf3",
                        "source_entry_id": "1f4735ba-0a71-36cf-b988-6cbc15860024",
                        "source_sense_id": "78496872-fc58-388f-a521-081873df2f94",
                        "target_lang_word": "incomprehensibility",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "incomprehensibility",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                        "entry_id": "265fcfc6-bfd6-39d1-98e2-654655ac452e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The condition of being incomprehensible."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1cece44d-c0e7-490d-8735-8258113a73a5",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "We should be quite willing to desist from the demand of a dogmatical answer to our questions, if we understood beforehand that, be the answer what it may, it would only serve to increase our ignorance, to throw us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into another still greater, and perhaps lead us into irreconcilable contradictions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5ac78b15-54ce-4671-8351-38e6d1ff3582",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "The king here alludes, as he does in various other passages, to the ancient Academicians, or Sceptics, who taught the uncertainty and incomprehensibility of truth."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bd4b0e4f-355d-42f7-bbf0-5c6e0be01b4d",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "The first principle of natural religion contains innumerable — I had almost said — impossibilities. hat is God? is^([sic]) involved in the most absoluteincomprehensibility. And yet we must either admit the principle, or embrace ten thousand absurdities and impossibilities."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6c0571ba-95c1-4902-b000-38d923aa2505",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "Why he had done it, what could have provoked him to such a breach of hospitality, and so suddenly turned all his partial regard for their daughter into actual ill-will, was a matter which they were at least as far from divining as Catherine herself : but it did not oppress them by any means so long; and, after a due course of useless conjecture, that “it was a strange business, and that he must be a very strange man,” grew enough for all their indig- nation and wonder; though Sarah, indeed, still indulged in the sweets of incomprehensibility, exclaiming and conjecturing with youthful ardour — “My dear, you give yourself a great deal of needless trouble,” said her mother at last; “depend upon it, it is something not at all worth under' standing.”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1609e67a-e257-43f4-b606-3dd7271e7326",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "He survived four days, in great suffering, and then died, leaving a case of perfect incomprehensibility to the world. Those who believe in spontaneous combustion ascribe it to electricity, and what not; others, again, are rather incredulous. The Baron de Liebig makes very merry over the fact of the nightcap being burned, and the hair on the head escaping scot-free."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "23f3b6a5-dc98-4565-9997-d53a18a8cd16",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "I well remember, that the event I am about to narrate, called forth, in several of the German periodical publications, the most passionate (and in more than one instance, blasphemous) declamations, concerning the incomprehensibility of the moral government of the world, and the seeming injustice and cruelty of the dispensations of Providence. But, assuredly, every one of my readers, however deeply he may sympathize with the poor sufferers, will at once answer all such declamations by the simple reflection, that no one of these awful events could possibly have taken place under a wise police and humane government, and that men have no right to complain of Providence for evils which they themselves are competent to remedy by mere common sense, joined with mere common humanity."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "17c17dcf-925c-40db-bd2f-e8cd6674e7b8",
                                                "sense_id": "370ce9df-54f2-36d5-b350-05e4f78f28e1",
                                                "text": "This speech naturally only increased the incomprehensibility of the whole conversation; and the commandant beginning, in rather haut en bas termsy to demand an explanation, like all cowards, when driven into a comer I became desperate."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6703bb54-eafc-368c-9aee-854a2dceb023",
                                        "entry_id": "265fcfc6-bfd6-39d1-98e2-654655ac452e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Something that cannot be understood."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b3f0672f-9c96-47ab-a640-d90d0ab689c5",
                                                "sense_id": "6703bb54-eafc-368c-9aee-854a2dceb023",
                                                "text": "That left Bradly as bemused as ever, for it posed another incomprehensibility: Why had Podson walked here?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
